<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Experimental Setup</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Datasets and Benchmarks.</span>
We design a series of experiments leveraging different components of our dataset and benchmarks:
<span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(1)</span> For the first experiment to evaluate semantic active perception (<a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 Semantic Active Perception Evaluation ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ SaPaVe: Towards Active Perception and Manipulation in Vision-Language-Action Models for Robotics" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sec.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>), we test models on our ActiveViewPose-200K, which is split into Train/Val/Test1/Test2.
In Test1, the language instructions contain explicit positional indicators (<em id="S4.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g.</em>, “on the left”), whereas in Test2, the instructions omit detailed camera movements, requiring the model to infer them from both the text and the image.
<span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(2)</span> For the second experiment to evaluate the effect of fixed or dynamic cameras across different types (<a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3 Fixed and Dynamic Cameras Evaluation. ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ SaPaVe: Towards Active Perception and Manipulation in Vision-Language-Action Models for Robotics" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sec.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>), we assess performance on ActiveManip-Bench (see <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 ActiveViewPose-200K and ActiveManip-Bench ‣ 3 Method ‣ SaPaVe: Towards Active Perception and Manipulation in Vision-Language-Action Models for Robotics" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sec.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>), which comprises 6 task types: Unoccluded/Occluded/Out-of-View Pick-and-Place, as well as Unoccluded/Occluded/Out-of-View Articulated Manipulation.
<span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(3)</span> For All subsequent experiments (<a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3 Fixed and Dynamic Cameras Evaluation. ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ SaPaVe: Towards Active Perception and Manipulation in Vision-Language-Action Models for Robotics" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sec.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>, <a href="#S4.SS4" title="4.4 Comparison with existing VLA models ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ SaPaVe: Towards Active Perception and Manipulation in Vision-Language-Action Models for Robotics" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sec.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a>, <a href="#S4.SS6" title="4.6 Ablation Studies ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ SaPaVe: Towards Active Perception and Manipulation in Vision-Language-Action Models for Robotics" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sec.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.6</span></a>), we employ our real-robot teleoperated dataset, including 4 task categories: Occluded/Out-of-View Pick-and-Place, and Occluded/Out-of-View Articulated Manipulation.
See Appx. <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Real-World Experiments ‣ SaPaVe: Towards Active Perception and Manipulation in Vision-Language-Action Models for Robotics" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a> for details.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Baselines.</span>
We compare our method against several strong baselines:
<span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(1)</span> In the first experiment, we compare our model with current powerful VLM, including the general models (<em id="S4.SS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g.</em>, Qwen2.5-VL-72B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>, Gemini-2.5-Pro <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>), as well as the specialized spatial VLM (<em id="S4.SS1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g.</em>, Multi-SpacialMLLM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>]</cite>).
<span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(2)</span> In the second experiment, we adopt the same underlying architecture but explore different camera configurations:
(a) Fixed Camera: A standard VLA model that operates from a static camera—this is the most common setup in prior work. We use our model but keep the head camera fixed.
(b) Fixed Camera + Wrist Camera: We again use our model with a fixed head camera and introduce two wrist cameras.
(c) Active Camera + Wrist Camera: We use our model, combining the active head camera with two wrist cameras.
<span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(3)</span> Finally, in the third experiment, we compare our model with existing VLA baselines, <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> and GR00T-N1 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>, both of which are recognized for their strong general-purpose capabilities.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Evaluation Metrics.</span>
For all experiments, we report the success rate. For the first experiment, a prediction is considered successful if it falls within a tolerance of the ground-truth pitch and yaw changes (See Appx. <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Discussion ‣ SaPaVe: Towards Active Perception and Manipulation in Vision-Language-Action Models for Robotics" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T3" class="ltx_table">
